drvOms::motor_init	: This initializes the driver task and sets up the
			  data structures for the OMS cards.  Originally
			  iocInit failed because the messages sent to the
			  cards got out of synch with the replies.  Some
			  messages do not require replies but I found that I
			  had to force a wait by putting in a dummy receive
			  message call, which timed out, but allowed the real
			  replies to come back at the expected places.  The
			  sequence completes OK and reports that the motor_task
			  has started.  This task is supposed to process
			  queued user messages, presumably from record support.

iocInit then carries on with initializing record support, running down
the list of record support entry tables looking for init routines to
run.  In this system it does not appear to find any, which is fair
enough; recMotor does not have an init routine.  It does have init_record,
which is static and requires a pointer to a motorRecord structure as a 
parameter, so cannot be called from the VxWorks command line.

In the next stage, device support initialization, iocInit runs
devOms::oms_init which performs a card inventory.  There is also a
report that the grating CADs have been initialized successfully.
devOms::oms_init_record is called in a loop to find which OMS ports
have motors attached, and it finds them OK.  And that is as far as it
goes.  The fields in the motor records can be probed and they have
their default values but these cannot be changed; as soon as a value
is entered the field value snaps back to the default.

